WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a daily battle is being fought by more  sri lankans the battle to make ends meet  prices are rising so fast many are  wondering how they will feed their  families i'm working now where the  terror is not enough for us because all  the things up  gone up  prices are too high usually i buy 500  grams of each vegetable now it's just  250 grams  unsustainable debt reduced tourism  income and mismanagement and corruption  by the government has created sri  lanka's worst economic crisis in memory  the shortage of fuel is only adding to  the misery  it is difficult with the diesel problems  things are more expensive because of  this there are less people shopping  because many are still in fuel cues so  the vegetables don't sell and we have to  throw them away  with food so expensive people are being  forced to compromise the quantity has  come down than what it was  so and even the quality wise you buy the  cheaper stuff then the expensive  pensioners are badly affected with their  money buying far less  we normally don't cook at home  you see what we do is that we come and  buy something from a  restaurant and then go and take one  packet of lunch and then share  you see wife and mine  so  the living of this country is very  difficult  balancing their reducing purchasing  power with the need to put nutritious  meals on the table is one of the biggest  challenges facing sri lankans amid this  economic crisis that's a fact that prime  minister and acting president ranil  vikrama singha says he's hoping to  address  speaking to an international panel on  preventing global hunger and famine he  revealed the extent of the problem  the global crisis  and an internal crisis both  have come together  and  uh brought us  to a level where  many estimate as much as six million  are  facing  malnutrition  other reports which i have even say  seven and a half million vikrama singha  has admitted sri lanka is bankrupt  so whoever parliament votes in as a  successor to gotab rajapaksa will have  to solve the country's economic crisis  before it becomes a humanitarian one  miner fernandez al-jazeera colombo  sri lanka is appealing to friendly  countries for help with its flagging  economy india has extended a new credit  line worth 55 million dollars for  fertilizer imports china has announced  assistance worth 73 million dollars for  the supply of essential goods but has  stayed silent on sri lanka's request to  defer the loan repayment  colombo is also seeking an imf bailout  package to tide over the worst economic  crisis since independence  iran ramaratni is a sri lankan member of  parliament and the former state minister  of finance he says the country needs  both short and long-term financial  support the immediate challenges are  there are full queues running into days  and days  people on the streets trying to get a  few liters of petroleum  then there were gas  shortages cooking gas shortages and then  pharmaceuticals food and fertilizer  if you were to quantify you need about  roughly about  400 million dollars a month on full and  about another 100 to 150 million dollars  on gas pharmaceuticals food and  fertilizer so this is where assistance  is needed through maybe supplies credits  and also friendly countries  actually existing that is to take care  of the immediate issues on the streets  in colombo  the economic issue is much wider as  there is a discussion with the imf i  think with the new government in place  next week i would expect to sign an  agreement a staff level agreement with  the imf within weeks that would be the  beginning of actually trying to turn  things around  i think the impact of the imf is  certainly not instant it's more  long-term it will give a signal to the  market because we have been cut away  from the debt markets and then actually  to get an imf program approved by the  imf board it might take several months  but this is only the beginning so  meanwhile we have to appeal  we are working towards an extended fund  facility retirement but we have to  appeal for more emergency funding once  we have got the staff level agreement in  place

